Package: bayescompR
Title: A Pure R Implementation of the Stan Probabilistic Programming Language
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R:
    person("Adam R", "Sophie", email = "adamrsophie@gmail.com",
           role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"))
Description: An independent implementation, written entirely in R, of the
    Stan probabilistic programming language and its inference algorithms.
    Stan programs are tokenized, parsed into a typed abstract syntax tree,
    checked by a semantic analyzer, and compiled into R closures that run on
    a reverse mode automatic differentiation tape. Constrained parameters are
    mapped to an unconstrained space with the Jacobian adjustments used by
    Stan. Inference includes the No-U-Turn sampler with multinomial
    trajectory sampling and windowed step size and metric adaptation, static
    Hamiltonian Monte Carlo, Riemannian manifold samplers, microcanonical
    Langevin Monte Carlo, the Metropolis adjusted Langevin algorithm,
    adaptive random walk Metropolis, slice and elliptical slice sampling,
    blocked Gibbs sampling, affine invariant and differential evolution
    ensembles, non-reversible parallel tempering, zig-zag and bouncy
    particle samplers, tempered sequential Monte Carlo, particle filters
    and particle MCMC for state space models, stochastic gradient MCMC,
    expectation propagation, nested sampling, reversible jump MCMC,
    integrated nested Laplace approximation, maximum a posteriori and
    maximum likelihood optimization, Laplace approximation, mean field,
    full rank and normalizing flow variational inference, Pathfinder,
    neural posterior estimation and diffusion based sampling. A typed,
    versioned intermediate representation lets separately installed native
    backends compute the log density and its gradient. Posterior summaries
    use
    rank normalized split R-hat and bulk and tail effective sample sizes.
    No C++ compiler, Stan installation, or external inference engine is
    required. This package is not affiliated with or endorsed by the Stan
    Development Team or NumFOCUS.
